          ],
          "grammarNotes": "L'ALFABETO ITALIANO — the 21 letters and their names:\n\na, bi, ci, di, e, effe, gi, acca, i, elle, emme, enne, o, pi, cu, erre, esse, ti, u, vu, zeta\n\nThe foreign letters j (i lunga), k (cappa), w (doppia vu), x (ics) and y (ipsilon) are not part of the Italian alphabet — they appear only in foreign words like 'taxi' or 'yogurt'.\n\nPRONUNCIATION — the sounds that change:\n\n• C and G before a, o, u are HARD: cane (KA-ne), gatto (GAT-to).\n• C and G before e, i are SOFT, like English 'ch' and 'j': cena (CHE-na), gelato (je-LA-to).\n• To keep the HARD sound before e, i, Italian adds an H: che (KE), chi (KI). So 'ch' always sounds like K, never like English 'church'.\n• GLI is like the 'lli' in 'million': famiglia, figlio.\n• GN is like the 'ny' in 'canyon': gnocchi, signora.\n• SC before e, i sounds like English 'sh': sci (SHEE), scena. Before a, o, u it stays 'sk': scuola (SKWO-la).\n\nSPELLING OUT LOUD: when someone asks 'Come si scrive?' (How is it written?), answer with the letter names one by one. Marco → emme, a, erre, ci, o.",
